-Together, DME and CAFR enhance defect morphology representation and its subsequent reconstruction across feature pyramid scales.
+In \model, CAFR replaces the upsampling operations at both top-down fusion stages, with upsampling factor $s=2$ and reassembly kernel size $k=5$. Its computation comprises two parallel paths followed by weighted aggregation: the weight-prediction path generates a normalized kernel for each output location, while the feature-extraction path obtains local neighborhoods from the original input. The predicted weights and source neighborhoods are aligned on the output grid before reassembly.
+
+\emph{1) Reassembly-weight prediction:}
+Given an input feature map $X\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}$, a $1\times1$ convolution first reduces its channel dimension:
+\begin{equation}
+X_c=E_{1\times1}(X).
+\label{eq:cafr-compression}
+\end{equation}
+Here, $X_c\in\mathbb{R}^{C_r\times H\times W}$, where $C_r=\max(\lfloor C/4\rfloor,16)$; $E_{1\times1}$ includes batch normalization and SiLU activation. A linear $3\times3$ convolution then predicts the unnormalized reassembly weights:
+\begin{equation}
+K'=E_{3\times3}(X_c).
+\label{eq:cafr-logits}
+\end{equation}
+The resulting tensor has shape $s^2k^2\times H\times W$. For $s=2$ and $k=5$, this corresponds to 100 weight channels at each input location.
+
+\emph{2) Weight alignment and normalization:}
+Pixel Shuffle rearranges $K'$ into a tensor of shape $k^2\times sH\times sW$, assigning a separate set of weight logits to each high-resolution output location. Softmax is then applied along the $k^2$ neighborhood dimension:
+\begin{equation}
+K_{i,j}=\operatorname{softmax}\!\left(
+[\operatorname{PS}(K')]_{:,i,j}\right).
+\label{eq:cafr-kernel}
+\end{equation}
+Each output location $(i,j)$ thus receives $k^2=25$ nonnegative weights that sum to one and correspond to the positions in a $5\times5$ input neighborhood. Pixel Shuffle acts on the predicted weights, not on the input features or the final output.
+
+\emph{3) Local feature extraction and reassembly:}
+In parallel, Unfold extracts a $k\times k$ neighborhood around every input location, using unit stride and zero padding of $(k-1)/2$. Let $P_{c,p,q,u}$ denote the feature value in channel $c$ at neighborhood offset $u$ around source location $(p,q)$. For a zero-based output location $(i,j)$, the corresponding source location is $(\lfloor i/s\rfloor,\lfloor j/s\rfloor)$. Weighted aggregation gives
+\begin{equation}
+Y_{c,i,j}=\sum_{u=1}^{k^2}K_{i,j,u}
+P_{c,\lfloor i/s\rfloor,\lfloor j/s\rfloor,u}.
+\label{eq:cafr-reassembly}
+\end{equation}
+The index $u$ enumerates neighborhood offsets in row-major order, consistently for the predicted weights and the unfolded patches. Output locations associated with the same source location share its input neighborhood but can use different predicted weights. These weights are shared across feature channels, producing $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times sH\times sW}$ rather than simply replicating individual input feature values.
+
+By predicting location-specific reassembly weights, CAFR adaptively aggregates local features during upsampling, helping preserve defect-related information in multi-scale feature fusion.
